        "target": "Given the problem, I identified the key quantities: Karen's paddling speed on a still pond (10 miles per hour), the river's current speed (4 miles per hour), and the river's length (12 miles). The unknown I needed to find was the time it would take Karen to paddle up the river against the current.\n\nFirst, I recognized that Karen's effective paddling speed against the current would be her still-water speed minus the river's current speed. This calculation was straightforward: \\(10 - 4 = 6\\) miles per hour. This step was necessary because the current of the river would slow Karen down, and I needed to account for this reduction in speed.\n\nNext, I used the formula for time, which is distance divided by speed. Substituting the known values, I calculated the time as \\( \\frac{12 \\text{ miles}}{6 \\text{ miles per hour}} = 2 \\text{ hours} \\). This calculation provided the time it would take Karen to paddle up the river.\n\nTo check the result, I considered the physical scenario: if Karen paddles at 6 miles per hour and the river is 12 miles long, it logically follows that it would take her 2 hours to cover the distance. This check aligns with the problem statement and confirms the solution.\n\nThe operation I would reuse is the calculation of effective speed by subtracting the current speed from the still-water speed, and then using the distance-speed-time relationship to find the time. This method is applicable whenever a vessel's speed is affected by a current or wind.\n\nFINAL: 2",
        "target_sha256": "47fba4b163c0318bf9552d0c77363d976e9cb892a0716f12ca5ec5d9993ec04d"
      }
