        "student_prefix_sha256": "81cbc9fee0dac57e159fa6c62e888c6062cbf2a805dceb26f568c16e233849d2",
        "target": "Given the problem, I identified the key quantities: the sewers' capacity (240,000 gallons) and the runoff produced per hour of rain (1,000 gallons). The unknown I needed to find was the number of days of rain the sewers could handle before overflowing.\n\nTo solve this, I first calculated the total number of hours of rain the sewers could handle by dividing the sewers' capacity by the runoff per hour:\n\\[ \\text{Hours of rain} = \\frac{240,000 \\text{ gallons}}{1,000 \\text{ gallons/hour}} = 240 \\text{ hours} \\]\n\nNext, I converted the hours of rain into days by dividing by the number of hours in a day (24 hours):\n\\[ \\text{Days of rain} = \\frac{240 \\text{ hours}}{24 \\text{ hours/day}} = 10 \\text{ days} \\]\n\nTo check the result, I reasoned that if the sewers can handle 240,000 gallons and each day of rain produces 24,000 gallons (1,000 gallons/hour * 24 hours/day), then 10 days of rain would produce 240,000 gallons, which matches the sewers' capacity.\n\nThe operation I would reuse is the conversion from hours to days, as it is a straightforward and reliable method for handling similar problems involving time units. I would apply this operation whenever I need to convert a total time in hours to days for a given context.\n\nFINAL: 10",
